WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.714
Type III covers low-pressure chillers. These are commercial systems — big
equipment, big consequences.

00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:51.429
Rupture discs are safety devices on low-pressure vessels that burst to relieve
pressure if it exceeds safe limits. They prevent catastrophic vessel failure.
Once a rupture disc bursts it must be replaced. Never plug or bypass a ruptured

00:00:51.429 --> 00:01:17.143
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:42.857
Rupture Disc: Safety device that bursts to relieve dangerous pressure. Must be
replaced after activation.

00:01:42.857 --> 00:02:08.571
Here's why this matters on the job: Always drain water from chiller tubes before
deep evacuation to prevent freeze damage.

00:02:08.571 --> 00:02:34.286
Watch out for these: Never bypass a ruptured disc. And — Drain water before
evacuating

00:02:34.286 --> 00:03:00.000
Why does a low-pressure system have air leaking in rather than refrigerant
leaking out? Think about the pressure relationship.

